                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   The round platform stood empty, with its ring of folding chairs
knocked askew, as if a congregation had risen in the middle of a sen‐
tence. We carried the stones to it together. Yanaisa had Yúcahu in both
arms. Manny walked beside the trolley with one hand clamped to his
ribs, like an usher escorting a very heavy bride. Rafa took the old palo.
Desmond held the case lid. Mikey and his mother came behind, close
together. A path opened in the crowd for us, and nobody told it to.
   The clock on the wall said 10:01.
   I set La Madre on the pedestal they had built for her. Yanaisa set
Yúcahu down beside her, an inch apart. Two small dark stones on a
white stage under a steel roof, in a hall made for armies. “They built a
place for one,” said Yanaisa. “They get two.”
   I could feel another clock, too. Not the one on the wall. The one in
every pocket in the borough: sixty-one thousand phones with a green
leaf lit on each, waiting to play the rain.
   “Ten-oh-five,” said Danny at my shoulder, small and gray-faced, the
bandage on his ear gone dingy. “It’s a hard schedule. Runs by itself,
from a server somewhere. I can’t stop it.” He swallowed. “I can only tell
you when.”
   Sofía touched his shoulder, once, the way you do for a person who
has done the bravest thing in the room and hasn’t noticed.
   Yanaisa looked at me. “When you’re ready, I’ll put my hand where
yours is.”
   I laid my left palm on La Madre. She was warm the way a rock is
warm at four in the afternoon, a held warmth, not a fever. I kept my
right hand in my pocket around the coin, which had been hers for five
hundred years and mine for thirteen.
   Yanaisa lifted Yúcahu with both hands and moved him across the
white stage, an inch at a time, toward his mother.
   They touched.
   There was no flash. There was no wind. There was a sound I had
heard in every dance hall in the Bronx since before I could walk: a
small, bright, wooden tock. Two sticks struck together. The clave. The
key. The pulse that every other rhythm hangs from, the thing the pat‐
tern is built around.



